<h2 id="h-cam-systems-and-forgiveness-in-the-field">The EKO Cam and Forgiveness in the Field</h2>
<p>The Whitetail Maxx runs Bear&rsquo;s EKO cam system. Its job is to balance speed with a draw cycle that hunters can manage from awkward positions. Combined with the 6.5-inch brace height, the result is a bow that leans toward forgiveness rather than raw speed.</p>
<p>Brace height is the distance from the grip to the string at rest. A taller brace height like 6.5 inches means the arrow leaves the string sooner, which reduces the effect of small form errors such as grip torque or a slightly collapsed bow arm. Hunters rarely shoot with perfect form, so this extra margin helps arrows land where you aimed.</p>
<?= callout('tip', 'Draw length matters most', 'The Whitetail Maxx adjusts from 26" to 30". A draw length that is even half an inch off will hurt your accuracy more than any spec on this page. If you are unsure of yours, measure it before ordering.') ?>
<p>If you own an older bow and want to understand how draw length changes work, our guide to the <?= post_link('2016-pse-draw-length-adjustment', '2016 PSE draw length adjustment') ?> explains cam modules, charts and when to use a bow press. The principles carry over to most modern cams.</p>

<h2 id="h-accuracy-beyond-the-range">Accuracy Beyond the Range</h2>
<p>A bow that groups well on a calm afternoon at the range has to do the same on a frosty November morning. The Whitetail Maxx helps here with its forgiving geometry, but your habits close the gap:</p>
<ul class="checks">
  <li>Practice from an elevated platform or a stand in your backyard, not only from flat ground.</li>
  <li>Shoot in the bulky clothes you will wear in late season. They can change your anchor and string clearance.</li>
  <li>Take one cold, first-shot-of-the-day arrow each session. That is the shot you will get on a deer.</li>
  <li>Practice from a seated position, especially if you hunt from a ground blind.</li>
</ul>
<p>The included Fatal 4-pin sight covers typical whitetail distances well. Sight in pins at even intervals that match your hunting spots, such as 20, 30, 40 and 50 yards.</p>

<h2 id="h-arrow-selection-and-energy-transfer">Arrow Selection and Energy Transfer</h2>
<p>The RTH package gets your bow ready, but arrows are on you. Choose a shaft spine that matches your draw weight and draw length, and pick a total arrow weight that balances speed and penetration. For whitetails, a moderate arrow weight with a sharp broadhead is plenty.</p>
<p>The Whisker Biscuit-V rest fully supports the arrow, so it will not fall off the rest when you move in a stand. That makes it a favorite for hunters. It does contact the fletching, so check that your vanes clear cleanly and do not tear over time. If you later want more clearance, a <?= amz('drop away arrow rest compound bow', 'drop-away arrow rest') ?> is a common upgrade.</p>
<p>For broadheads, start with a dependable <?= amz('mechanical broadheads 100 grain', 'mechanical broadhead') ?> for large cutting diameter on deer, or a fixed blade if you prefer simplicity. Always confirm broadheads group with your field points before the season.</p>

<h2 id="h-noise-vibration-and-shot-reaction">Noise, Vibration and Shot Reaction</h2>
<p>Deer react to sound. A loud, buzzy shot gives a whitetail time to drop and spin before the arrow arrives. The Whitetail Maxx RTH includes a snubnose stabilizer that helps absorb vibration and quiet the shot, and the compact frame keeps the bow balanced in your hand.</p>
<p>To keep it quiet over time, wax your string regularly, tighten every accessory screw, and add string silencers or limb dampeners if you hear rattles. The 5-spot quiver should be snug, since a loose quiver is one of the most common sources of noise on any hunting bow.</p>

